CONCERNING 0x2770:0x9060 CAMERAS

This camera identifies itself as "USB Digital Still Camera" and is sold as
"Dual Mode Camera 1300"; usb.ids calls it "NHJ, Ltd A130". It uses the same
command set as the 0x2770:0x905C cameras (see README.905C), so the ID block,
the catalog and the photo data are read in the same way. What differs is the
format of the large photos.

Features:

	-- 1280x960 and 640x480 resolution settings
	-- photos are saved in volatile SDRAM, so don't let the batteries die
	-- also works as a webcam; that mode is not supported here

Catalog entries:

	byte 0       0x67 for a 1280x960 photo, 0x76 for a 640x480 photo
	bytes 1-2    0x02 0x80 (640, the width of one quadrant, big-endian)
	bytes 4-6    size of the photo data, little-endian
	byte 11      lighting, used for white balance as on the other cameras

The 0x76 photos are a single 640x480 stream in the usual compressed format,
exactly as on the 0x905C cameras.

The 0x67 photos are stored as four 640x480 quadrants:

	bytes 0-15   four 32-bit little-endian sizes, one per quadrant, in the
	             order top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right
	bytes 16-31  constant on every photo seen (ff bb ff ff fb d9 fb ff
	             ff db 7f 7f ff ff bf ff), not used
	byte 32-     the four quadrants, back to back

Each quadrant is a complete 640x480 image in the usual compressed format,
with its own fresh predictors. The quadrants together form one 1280x960
Bayer image, which then goes through the usual demosaic and postprocessing.
The header plus the four sizes adds up exactly to the size in the catalog.

Tested with one camera (firmware ID string 2004072818), 15 photos in both
resolutions.
